{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S231-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"8e5e28d4e99d831933c66e7a888f2d0a6b29b83934292ce60fccdba392f3ef3d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 makes a clean inspection evidence of concealment; A5 treats thoroughness as proof of greater danger.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's 'nothing wrong' produces A3's hiding theory; B4's double check produces A5's escalation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 defend the inspection, but B has no concrete stake in accepting its result.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 demands a more serious inspection because B4 establishes that ordinary checking was thorough.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes an inspection but never identifies the inspected place, object, or practical decision.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's problem becoming 'good at hiding' economically converts reassurance into threat.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 85\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The inspection passed? That's suspiciously convenient.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They found nothing wrong.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then the problem has become good at hiding.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They checked everything twice.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Whatever escaped that deserves a more serious inspection.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats each reassuring finding as evidence that the underlying threat has become harder to detect.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General pessimism expects bad results; this card converts good results into affirmative evidence of a transformed threat.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 infers concealment from B2's clean result, and A5 infers a more formidable hidden problem from B4's thoroughness.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a distrusts institutional checking and demands more investigation; alternative b interprets social approval as coordinated preparation.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original meets the threshold and every hard gate: B2 and B4 each supply the reassurance that causes A's next inference. Internal rereading supports retention without invented changes.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The unnamed inspection and B's limited personal stake make this a narrow pass, despite unusually clear card logic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S231-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"83bf81be1491e2bf5578bcdf39a5c7736dfc104789f8f61a7e44f4b6f36daf50\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 converts direct praise into 'coordinated reassurance,' but A3 mainly questions how praise was delivered.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4 directly corrects A3 and causes A5; B2 does not establish A3's assumption that neighbours avoided A.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 reassures and B4 corrects, but B has no purpose beyond disputing A's suspicion.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 expands an individual concern into coordinated behaviour after B4 confirms multiple direct reassurances.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 identifies neighbours and a speech, giving the exchange an immediately understandable social setting.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'Coordinated reassurance' succinctly repurposes B4's correction.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The neighbours liked my speech? I thought they'd vote me off the committee.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They've asked you to chair next month's meeting. Please say yes; I'm exhausted.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"So they're keeping me where they can watch me.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll sit beside you and handle complaints.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"They've given me a minder. Will you be reporting my mistakes to them?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats each reassuring social development as evidence that exclusion has changed into surveillance.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary insecurity doubts praise; this card uses praise and assistance as positive evidence of a newly shaped threat.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 turns B2's invitation into surveillance, and A5 turns B4's supportive presence into supervision.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a seeks stricter external inspection; revised b makes an exhausted colleague's helpful offers evidence of personal monitoring.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision gives B a concrete need and makes each offer change the imagined threat. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's offer to sit beside A specifically causes A5's minder inference. The committee setting is newly illustrative.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The surveillance interpretation is deliberately extreme; A needs to play it as a sincere protective inference rather than a knowing accusation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S232-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a8ef4f8bf5883adf76942465c3901e6edf2a4645aded7e8ed890daa4778d9995\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 claims protective expertise; A3 searches for remaining work; A5 loses assurance when usefulness disappears.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's completed tightening causes A3's checking; B4's invitation without work causes A5's hesitation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B4 offers companionship instead of dependence, materially changing how A can participate.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 exposes the insecurity beneath A1's confident intervention after B4 removes the repair role.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's bike and bolts establish a concrete pre-ride situation actors can play immediately.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'check the checking' earns its absurdity through B2's completed maintenance.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 95\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Let me fix your bike before you ride. I know which bolts matter.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I've tightened them already.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll check the checking. There must be something left.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You can just come riding with me.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Without a repair to do? Give me a minute to remember how.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A takes charge of B's safety, then reveals increasing uncertainty as B removes the need for rescue.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple controlling behaviour preserves authority; this card exposes confidence that depends on another person's dependence.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3's 'There must be something left' and A5's difficulty accepting an ordinary ride reveal need beneath the expertise.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a lets practical authority dissolve into vulnerable companionship; alternative b bargains for a useful role within a celebration.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Both partner turns cause a distinct response, and the helpful B4 produces the clearest revelation of A's dependence. Internal rereading confirms the original passes unchanged.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 is slightly literary, and its heightening is emotional exposure rather than a larger protective intervention.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S232-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"6c2ef467ae6f5a04b2ce1159933282e603274489d71a9b746e7b536c010232ae\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 takes over difficulty; A3 invents exclusive follow-up work; A5 admits being steadier when useful.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's successful call causes A3's follow-up claim; B4's celebration causes A5's setup request.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4 actively asks for shared pleasure, offering A a relationship beyond managing problems.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 carries the need to be necessary into B4's problem-free celebration.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1's 'difficult phone call' leaves its recipient and stakes unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's request that celebration require help efficiently converts B4's solution into the same dependency.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 88\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll handle the difficult phone call for you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I've made it. It went well.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"There may be a follow-up only I can manage.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'd rather celebrate with you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Could the celebration require help setting up? I'm better when useful.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A tries to protect B through exclusive competence, then seeks a substitute task when B's independence undermines that confidence.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Habitual helpfulness offers useful labour freely; this card reveals discomfort when there is no labour left to provide.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 insists on work 'only I can manage,' while A5 asks B to make the celebration require assistance.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a pauses at the prospect of equal companionship; alternative b actively negotiates a new service role inside that companionship.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original passes because B's success and invitation cause two different attempts to remain necessary. Internal rereading confirms retention without requiring a more argumentative B.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The phone call is generic, and 'I'm better when useful' explains the emotional mechanism more directly than the bike alternative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S233-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ca65fa6ea01278020aaed07146ed767ffc44b16e2cdccefa059ca998466400e1\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 buys acceptance through dress; A3 invents a revised identity; A5 defends that newly acquired membership identity.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's casual dress code causes A3's reinterpretation; B4's removal suggestion threatens the identity defended in A5.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B1 is absent, but A1 establishes B as the adviser; B4 then offers a practical route out of the problem.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 makes removing a jacket a threat to the kind of member A has just become.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a club visit and a purchased formal jacket, though the club remains unnamed.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's plan to wear a formal jacket casually follows directly from B2's correction.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I bought the formal jacket you said would fit in at the club.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Tonight's casual.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then I'll become someone who wears a formal jacket casually.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You can take it off.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Not immediately. I've only just learned what kind of member this makes me.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A rapidly adapts to gain acceptance, then protects the identity created by that adaptation when B offers another adjustment.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Sunk-cost stubbornness protects a purchase; this card protects the newly imagined self that the purchase created.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 becomes a particular kind of person, and A5 explicitly protects a kind of member rather than the jacket's expense.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a improvises a public membership identity from dress advice; alternative b defends a friendship identity already announced to family.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original passes with two clear causal links and a practical, helpful B4 that creates further identity defence. Internal rereading supports retention.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 names its identity problem quite explicitly; a sincere delivery is needed to keep it from sounding like commentary on the card.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S233-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"f7517c59ac9c9e15f77ac26a3676c85d63981ba486558ce0b85a6ad3a63d0dd4\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 adapts to B's desired friendship; A3 defends being a climber; A5 adds reliability to that identity defence.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's cafe correction triggers A3's public commitment; B4's cancellation demand triggers A5's reliability objection.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants a modest shared outing and B4 resists being committed to climbing lessons.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 turns cancelling one activity into another damaging identity change after B4's practical suggestion.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's booked climbing lessons and A3's announcement to a sister make the commitment concrete.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'one identity long enough to belong' explains the card after the sharper reliability objection.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You wanted a more adventurous friend, so I've booked us climbing lessons.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I meant trying a new cafe.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Well, I'm a climbing person now. I've told my sister.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Cancel the lessons.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"And become unreliable as well? I'm trying to keep one identity long enough to belong.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A changes behaviour to fit B's preferred friend, then treats undoing that change as damage to the new self.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Overenthusiastic misunderstanding explains the excessive booking but does not explain defending a newly announced identity.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3's 'I'm a climbing person now' and A5's fear of becoming unreliable show identity defence beyond misreading 'adventurous.'\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a adapts in the moment to club etiquette; alternative b has already made a shared booking and public family declaration.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Despite an explanatory final clause, the original clears the rubric and hard gates. B4's cancellation proposal specifically creates A5's fear of a second undesirable identity, so internal rereading supports retention.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The final clause is more self-aware than ordinary conversation, and B's cancellation demand leaves less warmth than the jacket alternative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S234-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"e62cac90710e07a355b4ce77b06adb900f47992224e09410d5daf21f33825b97\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 minimizes six weekends of labour; A3 plants dated invoices; A5 disguises attribution as factual accuracy.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's credit produces A3's denial plus records; B4's initials question causes A5's accuracy defence.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 willingly recognizes A, while B4 challenges why further attribution has been engineered.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 defends the systematic initials exposed by B4 while preserving the modest pose.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's roof and six weekends, followed by A3's dated invoices, supply concrete playable evidence.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's self-fixing roof and six-weekend ladder plant the contradiction economically.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 92\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The roof practically fixed itself. My ladder was only there six weekends.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You did a lot of work.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Nothing worth mentioning, beyond the invoices I've arranged by date.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Why are your initials on every page?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"So the evidence doesn't embarrass us by becoming inaccurate.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A performs modesty while quietly assembling dated and attributed facts that establish personal importance.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A simple humblebrag advertises an achievement; this card plants an accumulating evidentiary trail while denying the need for credit.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 supplies duration, A3 supplies organized invoices, and B4 reveals initials that A5 defends as accuracy.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a builds a documentary record of practical labour; alternative b channels a numerical record toward public ceremonial recognition.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The evidence accumulates through the exchange, and B4 makes A defend its attribution without abandoning modesty. Internal rereading confirms the original passes unchanged.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's reference to evidence is a little conspicuous for someone supposedly letting the facts speak quietly.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S234-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"9176390d72fd873aeb0be59121aa87defa1d9b37afab63159b35465622fb5aba\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 minimizes thirty-seven calls; A3 implies additional attempts; A5 supplies documentation while denying a request for praise.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's numerical surprise causes A3's qualification; B4's speech accusation causes A5's call-log manoeuvre.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4 recognizes and names A's desired recognition, forcing A to pursue it indirectly.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A5 turns B4's accusation into a practical route for getting the evidence into a thank-you speech.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 identifies concert organization and thirty-seven calls; A5 makes the log a usable object.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3's exclusion of attempts enlarges the achievement under cover of restraint; A5 repeats that same logic.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 97\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Anyone could have organised the concert. My thirty-seven calls were hardly heroic.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Thirty-seven?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Only the completed ones. I wouldn't inflate the figure with attempts.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You want a thank-you speech.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"No, no. Just make sure whoever writes it has the call log.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A understates effort while providing increasingly verifiable facts that can secure recognition without an explicit claim to importance.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Fishing for compliments merely solicits praise; this card supplies records that make praise appear compelled by objective evidence.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 carefully distinguishes completed calls from attempts, and A5 routes the actual log to a potential speechwriter.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a protects accurate attribution on existing invoices; alternative b exploits B's accusation to arrange future public acknowledgment.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original is especially economical: B4's proposed speech becomes A5's means of placing evidence. Internal rereading confirms retention with no necessary rewrite.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B2 is largely a prompt for more information, and A5 assumes a speechwriter may exist rather than establishing one.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S235-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"05cfa90c935e44ca23f00368df14d9b6835deb85d3d69b72320c9ae270424dd4\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 requests taps and A5 changes their meaning, but A3 explains historical meanings without making the action recur.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's luck question produces A3's private explanation; B4's strong tea causes A5's memorial reinterpretation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 seeks entry into a family custom and B4 challenges its relevance to the tea being served.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 moves from a complaint about tea to missing Mum because B4 removes the original complaint.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's cup taps and A3's weak tea make a clear domestic ritual.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's guest-facing luck explanation contrasts economically with the family's complaint signal.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 80\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Wait, don't drink yet. Two taps on the cup first.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"For luck? That's good tea.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Two taps meant Mum had made it weak. We'd better tap again; she'll think we've gone polite.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I miss her complaints. The table's too quiet.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Two more, then. These are to leave her a turn. We can wait before we drink.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A insists on a recurring cup-tapping ritual whose purpose changes from unexplained necessity to teasing continuity to room for remembrance.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Explaining a family custom can leave its meaning fixed; this card renews the same action with a different purpose on each return.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 withholds drinking for taps, A3 requests them again to preserve teasing, and A5 requests another return to leave Mum a turn.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a develops an intimate family ritual through shared grief; revised alternative b makes a rehearsal ritual solve changing practical group needs.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision preserves the tea setting while making recurrence and necessity explicit in spoken dialogue. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's discomfort with silence causes A5 to turn the taps into space for the absent person.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The action is requested through speech rather than separately narrated; performers must let the ritual breathe without treating the pauses as a compulsory scene ending.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S235-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ba62d2c14c69f9b828cc1c960454eca02023ecae877e6bd601ef6d4f87a1d48f\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 describes a recurring chair placement, but A3 and A5 mostly explain it rather than return to the action.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 supplies welcoming lateness for A3 to darken; B4 removes lateness, though A5 introduces a previously unmentioned loss.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 ask about the ritual but have no expressed rehearsal need or personal response to the absent member.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 introduces bereavement after B4's attendance correction, supplying gravity more than developed interaction.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a rehearsal room, a chair and a door, making the staging understandable.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 economically turns a welcome into a public reminder of lateness.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 68\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before rehearsal, turn that chair toward the door. We always do.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I've turned it back. Everyone's here.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Turn it toward the door again. Today it means no more interruptions.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The new singer's outside. She thinks it's blocking her way.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Turn it toward the door once more, beside mine. Now it's her welcome. We can't start without that.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A insists on repeating the same chair orientation while changing its meaning to meet each new rehearsal circumstance.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A symbolic object can change meaning while remaining untouched; this card makes the repeated action itself essential and newly meaningful.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 establishes the required turning, A3 repeats it as a boundary, and A5 repeats it as a welcome after B4 reveals exclusion.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a makes recurrence sustain an absent family member's place; alternative b transforms an obstructive rehearsal custom into inclusion of a newcomer.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revised rehearsal introduces a new singer and removes the unsupported bereavement turn. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 4, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 88. B4's report of exclusion causes A5 to reposition and reinterpret the same ritual as welcome.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The chair moves beside A as well as turning, and 'everyone's here' reflects B's mistaken attendance assumption; both details require clear delivery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S236-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"2b25f08917c86cacdd49a7f8dd7bc28e0777ba5a4786588b8ec4550a9faeec52\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 selects the crack and A3 enlarges it through the piano, but A5 returns to the initial warning without a larger disaster.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's leaning piano; A5's instruction to listen barely engages B4's need to stop the concert.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 wants an urgent intervention, while A5 offers a retrospective rebuke rather than a developed response.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 revisits what happened 'this morning' instead of expanding the consequence established by B2.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's stage crack and B2's leaning piano establish a concrete concert problem.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's musical personification is apt, but A5's 'auditioned its objection' repeats ornament instead of developing the situation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 59\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That little crack beside the piano was the stage warning us.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The piano's leaning. Help me move it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The crack's opened a place for the whole piano. Keep your end away from it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The front row's tilting too. I'm clearing the hall.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"It's reached the audience. That little crack is taking the whole concert downstairs. I'll get the balcony.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A reads each widening structural failure as a larger expression of the same early crack's warning.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Saying 'I told you so' vindicates an earlier opinion; this card repeatedly scales one early detail into the developing disaster.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 connects the crack to the piano, and A5 connects B4's tilting front row to the entire concert following it.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a now participates in a physical emergency; alternative b interprets administrative wedding mistakes through expanding identity confusion.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision retains the stage setting and introduces spreading damage plus shared evacuation work. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 5, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 95. B4's front-row movement causes A5 to enlarge the crack's reach to the audience and take responsibility for another area.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The structural escalation is theatrical compression; the scene needs sincere urgency to avoid becoming a sequence of decorative disaster metaphors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S236-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"b438035e311916edf9fa50e0e92895582fa5ca53aab7f20b071d20b1c6a4109b\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 selects a misspelled invitation; A3 and A5 read cake and venue confusion as enlargements of that error.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's wrong names and A5 uses B4's double booking, although misspelling and duplicate booking differ literally.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 bring consequential wedding updates but establish little personal objective beyond reporting them.